==== Triple Attack ====

'''Current Legends observation: Triple Attack does not occur from the offhand.'''

The calculator therefore allows Triple Attack only on the primary hand and on two-handed attack rounds.

{| class="eoTable2" style="width:70%"
|-
! Class !! Minimum Level !! Maximum Skill
|-
| '''[[Monk]]''' || 46 || 100
|-
| '''[[Warrior]]''' || 46 || 100
|-
| '''[[Rogue]]''' || 46 || 100
|-
| '''[[Ranger]]''' || 46 || 100
|-
| '''[[Berserker]]''' || 46 || 100
|}

The current working chance is:

<pre>
Triple Attack Chance =
    Triple Attack Skill
    ÷ (Triple Attack Skill + 800)
</pre>

Triple Attack is modeled as being checked only after a successful Double Attack.

Therefore:

<pre>
Expected primary / 2H attacks per timer opportunity =
    1
    + P(Double Attack)
    + P(Double Attack) × P(Triple Attack)
</pre>

At 100 Triple Attack skill:

<pre>
100 ÷ (100 + 800)
= 11.11%
</pre>

That 11.11% is conditional on Double Attack already succeeding.

When Actual TA Skill is blank, the calculator provisionally models 5 skill points per level beginning at level 46, capped at 100. Entering the actual skill overrides this assumption.

==== Burst of Power / Flurry ====

Burst of Power is available as a calculator checkbox.

The current working interpretation is:

* Flurry is checked only after a successful Triple Attack.
* Burst of Power grants a '''15%''' chance to flurry.
* A successful flurry produces either one or two additional attacks.
* The calculator assumes a 50% chance of either outcome.

The expected flurry contribution per successful Triple Attack is:

<pre>
0.15 × (0.50 × 1 + 0.50 × 2)
= 0.225 extra attacks
</pre>

With Burst of Power enabled:

<pre>
Primary / 2H multiplier =
    1
    + P(DA)
    + P(DA) × P(TA) × 1.225
</pre>

The offhand receives Double Attack but does not receive Triple Attack or this Triple-Attack-triggered flurry in the current model:

<pre>
Offhand multiplier =
    1 + P(Double Attack)
</pre>

This creates a real post-level-46 disadvantage for the offhand relative to the primary hand and a two-handed weapon.

=== Shared Modifiers ===

A modifier that affects both compared setups by the same proportion does not change which setup wins a normalized comparison.

Ordinary haste is therefore omitted from the calculator when the same haste applies to all compared weapons.

STR and ATK are also omitted from the simple comparison because the calculator is intended as a target-neutral weapon comparison rather than a complete melee simulator. A full expected-DPS model would require the attacker's combat statistics, the target's mitigation and avoidance, weapon skills, and the damage-roll distribution.

== Working Legends Damage Bonus Model ==

EverQuest damage-bonus formulas have changed over time.

The older classic-era one-handed formula and historical two-handed lookup tables do '''not''' match observed EverQuest Legends damage bonuses well.

The calculator therefore uses a later/live EverQuest damage-bonus formula published by a Daybreak developer. This model closely matches the currently observed Legends values across a range of one-handed and two-handed weapon delays.

The working formula is:

<pre>
Damage Bonus =
    Hand Modifier
    × max(Character Level, Weapon Damage)
    × (min(Weapon Delay, 50) ÷ 40)
    × (Character Level ÷ 100)
</pre>

Where:

* '''1H Hand Modifier = 0.8'''
* '''2H Hand Modifier = 1.1'''
* Weapon delay contributes only up to '''50 delay'''.
* If weapon damage exceeds character level, weapon damage is used instead of character level for that portion of the formula.
* Damage bonus begins at level 28 in the reference implementation.

The exact Legends integer rounding rule has not yet been proven.

For weapon comparison, the calculator therefore uses the '''unrounded''' working Damage Bonus value internally:

<pre>
Modifier
× max(Level, Damage)
× (min(Delay, 50) ÷ 40)
× (Level ÷ 100)
</pre>

This avoids allowing a speculative rounding rule to determine which one-handed weapon should be placed in primary. The calculator may display an integer Damage Bonus estimate for readability, but the normalized comparison itself uses the raw modeled value.

=== Why Delay Matters ===

Unlike the older classic one-handed damage-bonus formula, the newer formula scales the damage bonus with weapon delay.

This explains why two one-handed weapons at the same character level can display different damage bonuses and why slower two-handed weapons can receive much larger bonuses.

Because delay contribution caps at 50:

* Increasing delay from 20 to 40 increases modeled damage bonus.
* Increasing delay from 50 to 70 does '''not''' increase the delay portion further.
* A weapon with damage greater than character level can still receive a larger bonus because the formula uses <code>max(Level, Damage)</code>.

